<h3><em><strong>De-infibulation</strong></em><em><strong> can be done</strong></em><em><strong>:</strong></em></h3>
<ul><li>antenatally</li><li>1st stage of labour</li><li>@ the time of delivery</li><li>Before or after C/S</li></ul>', 1) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET content=EXCLUDED.content, type=EXCLUDED.type;
INSERT INTO topics (id, chapter_id, title, description, "order", is_published, author, source) VALUES ('t-mm-01-022', 'chap-mm-01-01', '21. HIRSUTISM IN YOUNG WOMEN', 'Detailed medical reference and study guide on 21. HIRSUTISM IN YOUNG WOMEN.', 22, true, 'Dr. Farzana Muneer', 'Maternal Mind FCPS/MRCOG Textbook') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET title=EXCLUDED.title, description=EXCLUDED.description, is_published=true;
INSERT INTO content_blocks (id, topic_id, type, content, "order") VALUES ('cb-5414981f', 't-mm-01-022', 'html', '<ul><li>presence of Coarse hairs</li><li>5 - 15%</li><li>Can be Idiopathic - no underlying endocrine disorder</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Pathophysiology:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>total hair follicles 50million</li><li>scalp = 100,000 - 150,000</li><li>few follicles form after birth</li><li>decline after 40 years</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Types of hairs:</strong></h3>
<ul><li><strong>lanugo:</strong> Fetus - Shed in 4months after birth</li><li><strong>Villus</strong>: &lt;2cm, longer than lanugo - all over the  body - non pigmented</li><li><strong>terminal:</strong> coarse - scalp, eyebrow, eyelashes Axilla, Pubic areas.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Phases of Hair Growth:</strong></h3>
<ul><li><em><strong>An</strong></em><em><strong>agen:</strong></em> active. growing phase - determine length of hairs.</li><li><em><strong>Catagen:</strong></em> involuting, stop growing.</li><li><em><strong>Telogen:</strong></em> Resting - hairs shedding.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Independent of androgens:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>eyebrow, eyelashes occipital region, lateral side of skull.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Low levels of androgens:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>public hairs and axillary hairs</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Need high levels of androgens:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Chest, Back, Lower abdomen, Face, Upper thigh.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>CAUSES OF HIRSUTISM:</strong></h3>
<h3><strong>1.</strong><strong>PCOS</strong>:</h3>
<p>70-80% Cause of Hirsutism</p>
<ul><li>insulin Resistance -&gt; insulin levels increase androgen levels by Theca cells -&gt; SHBG</li><li>30 - 75% women with PCOS are OBESE.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>2.</strong><strong>Acanthosis Nigerica</strong><strong>n:</strong></h3>
<p>3% of women with androgen excess</p>
<ul><li>severe insulin Resistance.</li><li>can present with virilization.</li><li>difficult to treat.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>3.</strong><strong>Androgen Secreting tumors:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>rare - 1/300-1/1000 women with Hirsutism have Androgen secreting tumors.</li><li>suspect if:</li></ul>
<p>1. Rapid onset of symptoms.</p>
<p>2. virilization.</p>
<p>3. cushingoid features.</p>
<h3><strong>Diagnosis</strong> on clinical presentation:</h3>
<ul><li>suppression or / stimulation Tests are misleading.</li><li>&gt;50% Adrenal Tumor with hyperandrogenism is malignant.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>4.</strong><strong>CAH non classic:</strong></h3>
<p><strong> </strong>1.5 - 2.5% of hyperandrogenic women.</p>
<h3><strong>5.</strong><strong>Drugs:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Androgenic effects - danazol, Anabolic steroids.</li><li>Non-Androgenic effects</li><li>Phenytoin, minoxidil, diazoxide.</li><li>Streptomycin, psoralen, penicillamine.</li><li>High dose steroids.</li></ul>
<h3>6.<strong>Idiopathic:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>all other causes ruled out.</li><li>No hormonal abnormalities, normal periods, ovaries, and no evidence of Tumors.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Evaluation:</strong></h3>', 1) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET content=EXCLUDED.content, type=EXCLUDED.type;
INSERT INTO content_blocks (id, topic_id, type, content, "order") VALUES ('cb-2388d252', 't-mm-01-022', 'html', '<table><tbody><tr><td><strong>HISTORY</strong></td><td><strong>EXAMINATION</strong></td></tr><tr><td>drugs<br/>wt. changes<br/>change in facial contour.<br/>Family history like premature male balding<br/>DM</td><td>extent type &amp; pattern of hairs<br/>virilization signs<br/>thyroid enlargement signs<br/>galactorrhea<br/>Abdominopelvic masses<br/>obesity<br/>Cushingoid features<br/>Systemic illness</td></tr></tbody></table>', 2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET content=EXCLUDED.content, type=EXCLUDED.type;
INSERT INTO content_blocks (id, topic_id, type, content, "order") VALUES ('cb-3972be97', 't-mm-01-022', 'html', '<h3><strong>Diagnosis:</strong></h3>
<ul><li><em><strong>Subjective assessment </strong></em><em><strong>by Ferriman</strong></em><em><strong> Galway Score:</strong></em></li><li>0 – 4 on each area (0 no of hair, 4 Extensive hair)</li><li>Total 9 areas</li></ul>
<h3><strong>1:</strong><strong> Upper</strong><strong> </strong><strong>lips 2:</strong><strong> </strong><strong>Chin 3</strong><strong>:</strong><strong> </strong><strong>Chest 4</strong><strong>:</strong><strong> </strong><strong>abdomen 5</strong><strong>:</strong><strong> lower Abd &amp; pubic </strong><strong>area 6</strong><strong>:</strong><strong> upper back </strong></h3>
<ul><li><strong>7</strong><strong>:</strong><strong> thighs 8</strong><strong>:</strong><strong> Forearms 9</strong><strong>:</strong><strong> Lower back</strong></li><li>score &gt;6 =&gt; significant</li><li><em><strong>Severity of Hirsutism:</strong></em></li><li>Score 8-15 Mild</li></ul>
<p>without other features like Menstrual problem treat cosmetically.</p>
<ul><li>Score &gt;15 Mod – Severe</li><li>investigate the cause</li><li><strong>Free </strong><strong>Testosterone</strong><strong> &gt;</strong>1.5-2ng/dl =&gt; Neoplasm.</li><li><strong>17</strong><strong>α</strong><strong> Hydroxyprogesterone</strong>    in</li><li>(1). All Non classic CAH</li><li>(2). PCOS but &lt;200mg/l</li><li><strong>pelvic USG</strong> - PCOD, Neoplasm.</li><li><strong>DHEA sulphate</strong> - adrenal Source.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Management:</strong></h3>
<ul><li><em><strong>Reassurance.</strong></em></li><li><em><strong>wt loss &amp; life style.</strong></em></li><li>5-10% wt loss =&gt; 4-5% improvement in Hirsutism. within 6months</li><li>1st line in Obese PCOS @ least for 6months.</li><li><em><strong>Cosmetic Methods:</strong></em></li><li>Waxing: Discouraged - risk of folliculitis</li><li>Bleaching: useful</li><li>Plucking: Discouraged</li><li>Shaving: Blunt hairs</li><li>Depilatory creams: useful, chronic use, skin irritation.</li><li><em><strong>Physical Methods:</strong></em></li><li><strong>Electrolysis: </strong></li><li>destruction of individual hair follicle via electrode.</li><li>Expensive</li><li>18months to 4yrs r required to treat</li><li>painful</li><li>practical for limited areas of skin</li><li>cause burns, Depigmentation, skin scarring.</li><li><strong>laser </strong><strong>Photo thermolysis</strong><strong> or selective </strong><strong>Photo thermolysis</strong><strong>:</strong></li><li>Appropriate for light skinned women. As they require lower Radiation pulses. Dark women =&gt; laser with cooling devices.</li><li>Covers wider area.</li><li>Less painful.</li><li>Reduces hair density permanently by 30% after 3-4 sessions.</li><li>In PCOS    density of hirsutism by laser.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Hormonal Therapies:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Conversion of Terminal hairs to villus hairs.</li><li>Long t/m - requiring 9-12months</li><li><em><strong>COCP:</strong></em></li><li>Don''t reverse Hirsutism,   need for shaving by 50% &amp; inhibit Progression.</li><li>SHBG + inhibits LH hormone &amp;   Testosterone.</li><li>Drosperinone containing pills (Yasmin) more effective</li><li>risk of Endometrial Hyperplasia.</li><li>Cycle regularity</li><li><em><strong>Cyproterone acetate CA:</strong></em></li><li>Decrease LH =&gt; decrease    Testosterone &amp; Androstenedione</li><li>Act as an androgen Antagonist Peripherally.</li><li>Lower Ferriman Score by 15 – 40% within 6months,</li><li>Max effect 6 – 12months.</li><li><strong>Diane 35</strong> (EE 35mg, 2mg CA)</li><li>S/E: wt gain, Depression, fatigue, sexual dysfunction</li><li>Altered LFT’s (do LFT’s before CA), VTE more than 2nd generation COCP.</li><li><strong>Androcur</strong> high dose CA 50mg.</li><li>Given in women who r resistant to low dose CA along with EE for short term use.</li><li><em><strong>Spironolactone: </strong></em></li><li>Aldosterone Antagonist + mild Diuretics</li><li>Androgen inhibitor + structurally similar to progesterone.</li><li>1/20th efficiency for dihydrotestosterone receptor binding so high doses r required.</li><li>100mg/ day is superior to low dose CA 12.5mg/day &amp; Finasteride 5mg/day for up to 12months.</li><li><em><strong>Finasteride:</strong></em> inhibitor of 5alpha reductase.</li><li>Used for t/m of BPH.</li><li>Similar efficiency as CA but few S/E.</li><li>Feminization of female fetus</li><li><em><strong>Flutamide:</strong></em> Non-steroidal anti androgens, Block androgen receptors &amp; at high doses inhibit    production of androgens.</li><li>Used in t/m of CA prostate.</li><li>S/E: Greenish tinge to urine, Dry scalp and skin, abnormal LFT''s (LFT''s should be checked before t/m).</li><li>250 - 500mg/day</li><li><em><strong>Insulin Sensitizers:</strong></em></li><li>Improve hirsutism in PCOS</li><li>Effective in both Obese and lean women</li><li><em><strong>Cell cycle Inhibitors:</strong></em></li><li>Eflornithine inhibits ornithine decarboxylase in skin.</li><li>Topical use twice/day.</li><li>Rapidly absorbed (within 5min).</li><li>Reduce hair growth by 60%.</li><li>Stop if no benefit after 4months.</li></ul>
<p><strong>S/E:</strong> acne, photosensitivity.</p>
<ul><li><em><strong>GnRH-A</strong></em> loss of BMD 2-8% over 6months</li></ul>', 3) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET content=EXCLUDED.content, type=EXCLUDED.type;
INSERT INTO topics (id, chapter_id, title, description, "order", is_published, author, source) VALUES ('t-mm-01-023', 'chap-mm-01-01', '22. HMB IN ADOLESCENCE', 'Detailed medical reference and study guide on 22. HMB IN ADOLESCENCE.', 23, true, 'Dr. Farzana Muneer', 'Maternal Mind FCPS/MRCOG Textbook') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET title=EXCLUDED.title, description=EXCLUDED.description, is_published=true;
INSERT INTO content_blocks (id, topic_id, type, content, "order") VALUES ('cb-39327dd4', 't-mm-01-023', 'html', '<ul><li>Incidence is 4.8 – 37% depending upon definition, geographical location and socioeconomic status.</li><li>Adolescent cycles last between 2 – 7days and occurs every 21 – 25days.</li><li>50% cycle anovulatory in 1st year.</li><li>25% cycle anovulatory at 2 – 3year.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Pathological causes:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Bleeding disorders:</li><li><em><strong>Consider if:</strong></em></li><li>HMB from onset of menarche</li><li>Recurrent epistaxis.</li><li>Excessive brushing.</li><li>Prolong bleeding after injury</li><li>Fail to respond to conventional therapy.</li><li><strong>VWD:</strong> most common bleeding disorder in UK.</li><li><em><strong>Type I:</strong></em></li><li>Most common type</li><li>Milder</li><li>Quantitative deficiency of VW factor.</li><li><em><strong>Type II:</strong></em></li><li>Qualitative defects</li><li><em><strong>Type III:</strong></em></li><li>Severe form.</li><li>Association with stress.</li><li><strong>ITP:</strong></li><li>Most common platelet disorder in adolescence</li><li>7% present with HMB</li><li>Diagnosis on blood film</li><li><strong>Inher</strong><strong>ited Factor</strong><strong> </strong><strong>ⅸ</strong><strong> and VIII</strong><strong>:</strong></li><li>Consider in HMB in adolescence</li><li>Family history can be suggestive.</li><li><strong>Endocrinopathies:</strong></li><li><em><strong>Thyroid Disease:</strong></em></li><li>Hypothyroid important cause of HMB.</li><li>Symptoms of Hypothyroid + Heavy or irregular bleeding.</li><li><em><strong>PCOS:</strong></em></li><li>Diagnosis is challenging because irregular cycles, hyperandrogenism and multi – Follicular ovaries are common in adolescence</li><li><strong>Connective Tissue disorders:</strong></li><li>Ehler Danlos Syndrome</li><li>Childhood onset SLE.</li><li><strong>Endometriosis:</strong></li><li>Suspect when HMB is associated with dysmenorrhea and pelvic pain.</li><li><strong>Chronic systemic discase:</strong></li><li>Type I DM – treat HMB by optimizing Glucose levels.</li><li>Epilepsy:</li><li>Antiepileptic drugs cause HMB or irregular bleeding.</li><li>Sodium valproate, carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, lamotrigine.</li><li>Enzyme inducers reduce efficiency of COCP &amp; progesterone Carbamazepine, Phenytoin, phenobarbital rifampicin and high dose topiramate &gt; 200mg/ D &gt;&gt;Mirena or DMPA is suggested for those girls taking enzyme inducers.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Evaluation of Adolescent HMB:</strong></h3>
<h3><strong>History:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Age of menarche</li><li>Cycle length and regularity</li><li>Amount of blood loss.</li><li>Impact on quality of life</li><li>Associated symptoms</li><li>dysmenorrhea, dyschezia, bowel symptoms, wt changes, hirsutism, epistaxis, Bruising.</li><li>Sexual history</li><li>Risk of pregnancy, STI, contraception (age of partner and consent if active before age 15).</li><li>Address safe guarding issues.</li><li>Medical history</li><li>Bleeding disorders, any systemic disease.</li><li>Family history</li><li>Bleeding disorders, any systemic disease</li><li>VTE</li><li>Social history</li><li>School Absenteeism, bullying, self-confidence, relationships</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Examination:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Not routinely carried out.</li><li>Needed if effects of bleeding are suspected like anemia.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Investigations:</strong></h3>
<ul><li><em><strong>All girls with HMB:</strong></em></li><li>CBC</li><li>Coagulation screen</li><li>Fibrinogen.</li><li>PT.</li><li>APTT.</li><li><em><strong>Who fail to respond Medical Treatment or Pathology is suspected?</strong></em></li><li>TSH – if suspicion of hypothyroidism.</li><li>Prolactin</li><li>SHBG, free androgen index – if PCOS suspected.</li><li>Pregnancy Test</li><li>STI screen</li><li>Abdominal USG – to detect uterine anomalies or pelvic mass.</li><li>VWD screen – If suspected.</li><li>Factor VIII levels – if suspected.</li></ul>
<p>Table 4. Treatment</p>', 1) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET content=EXCLUDED.content, type=EXCLUDED.type;
