INSERT INTO content_blocks (id, topic_id, type, content, "order") VALUES ('cb-58b28374', 't-mm-05-014', 'html', '<ul><li>The management of women who present with problematic bleeding while using hormonal contraception is challenging.</li><li>Before starting hormonal contraception, women should be advised about the bleeding patterns expected both initially and in the longer term.</li><li>A pregnancy test is indicated for sexually active women using hormonal contraception who have problematic bleeding</li><li><em><strong>Clinically important bleeding patterns in women aged 15-44 years Scheduled bleedingMenstruation or regular withdrawal bleeding with combined hormonal contraception (CHC)Other bleeding patterns Frequent bleeding Infrequent bleeding Prolonged bleeding Spotting Breakthrough bleeding Amenorrhea More than five bleeding episodes within a reference periodFewer than three bleeding episodes within a reference periodBleeding episode lasting 14 days or moreVaginal discharge containing blood, that may not require the use of sanitary protectionUnscheduled bleeding in women using CHCNo bleeding or spotting days throughout the 90-day reference periodBleeding episode: one or more consecutive days of bleeding, bounded by bleed-free days. Reference period: a 90-day period of time during use of a hormonal contraceptive methodClinically important bleeding patterns in women aged 15-44 years Scheduled bleedingMenstruation or regular withdrawal bleeding with combined hormonal contraception (CHC)Other bleeding patterns Frequent bleeding Infrequent bleeding Prolonged bleeding Spotting Breakthrough bleeding Amenorrhea More than five bleeding episodes within a reference periodFewer than three bleeding episodes within a reference periodBleeding episode lasting 14 days or moreVaginal discharge containing blood, that may not require the use of sanitary protectionUnscheduled bleeding in women using CHCNo bleeding or spotting days throughout the 90-day reference periodBleeding episode: one or more consecutive days of bleeding, bounded by bleed-free days. Reference period: a 90-day period of time during use of a hormonal contraceptive method</strong></em>Clinically important bleeding patterns in women aged 15-44 years <strong>Scheduled bleeding</strong>Menstruation or regular withdrawal bleeding with combined hormonal contraception (CHC)<strong>Other bleeding patterns </strong>Frequent bleeding Infrequent bleeding Prolonged bleeding Spotting Breakthrough bleeding Amenorrhea More than five bleeding episodes within a reference periodFewer than three bleeding episodes within a reference periodBleeding episode lasting 14 days or moreVaginal discharge containing blood, that may not require the use of sanitary protectionUnscheduled bleeding in women using CHCNo bleeding or spotting days throughout the 90-day reference period<em><strong>Bleeding episode:</strong></em> one or more consecutive days of bleeding, bounded by bleed-free days. <em><strong>Reference period:</strong></em> a 90-day period of time during use of a hormonal contraceptive methodClinically important bleeding patterns in women aged 15-44 years <strong>Scheduled bleeding</strong>Menstruation or regular withdrawal bleeding with combined hormonal contraception (CHC)<strong>Other bleeding patterns </strong>Frequent bleeding Infrequent bleeding Prolonged bleeding Spotting Breakthrough bleeding Amenorrhea More than five bleeding episodes within a reference periodFewer than three bleeding episodes within a reference periodBleeding episode lasting 14 days or moreVaginal discharge containing blood, that may not require the use of sanitary protectionUnscheduled bleeding in women using CHCNo bleeding or spotting days throughout the 90-day reference period<em><strong>Bleeding episode:</strong></em> one or more consecutive days of bleeding, bounded by bleed-free days. <em><strong>Reference period:</strong></em> a 90-day period of time during use of a hormonal contraceptive method</li><li><em><strong>Points to cover in the clinical history from a woman using hormonal contraception who presents with problematic bleeding Clinical history taking should include an assessment of a woman''s Own concerns Current method of contraception and duration of use" Current method of contraception and duration of use" Compliance with the current contraceptive method Use of any medications (including over the counter preparations) which may interact with the contraceptive method illness/condition that may affect absorption of orally administered hormones Cervical screening history Risk of sexually transmitted infections (le. those aged &lt;25 years, or at any age with a new partner, or more than one partner in the last year) Bleeding pattern before starting hormonal contraception, since starting and currently Other symptoms suggestive of an underlying cause (eg. abdominal or pelvic pain, postcoital bleeding, and dyspareunia. heavy menstrual bleeding) Possibility of pregnancy compliance with the current contraceptive method Points to cover in the clinical history from a woman using hormonal contraception who presents with problematic bleeding Clinical history taking should include an assessment of a woman''s Own concerns Current method of contraception and duration of use" Current method of contraception and duration of use" Compliance with the current contraceptive method Use of any medications (including over the counter preparations) which may interact with the contraceptive method illness/condition that may affect absorption of orally administered hormones Cervical screening history Risk of sexually transmitted infections (le. those aged &lt;25 years, or at any age with a new partner, or more than one partner in the last year) Bleeding pattern before starting hormonal contraception, since starting and currently Other symptoms suggestive of an underlying cause (eg. abdominal or pelvic pain, postcoital bleeding, and dyspareunia. heavy menstrual bleeding) Possibility of pregnancy compliance with the current contraceptive method </strong></em>Points to cover in the clinical history from a woman using hormonal contraception who presents with problematic bleeding <em><strong>Clinical history taking should include an assessment of a woman''s </strong></em>Own concerns Current method of contraception and duration of use" Current method of contraception and duration of use" Compliance with the current contraceptive method Use of any medications (including over the counter preparations) which may interact with the contraceptive method illness/condition that may affect absorption of orally administered hormones Cervical screening history Risk of sexually transmitted infections (le. those aged &lt;25 years, or at any age with a new partner, or more than one partner in the last year) Bleeding pattern before starting hormonal contraception, since starting and currently Other symptoms suggestive of an underlying cause (eg. abdominal or pelvic pain, postcoital bleeding, and dyspareunia. heavy menstrual bleeding) Possibility of pregnancy compliance with the current contraceptive method Points to cover in the clinical history from a woman using hormonal contraception who presents with problematic bleeding <em><strong>Clinical history taking should include an assessment of a woman''s </strong></em>Own concerns Current method of contraception and duration of use" Current method of contraception and duration of use" Compliance with the current contraceptive method Use of any medications (including over the counter preparations) which may interact with the contraceptive method illness/condition that may affect absorption of orally administered hormones Cervical screening history Risk of sexually transmitted infections (le. those aged &lt;25 years, or at any age with a new partner, or more than one partner in the last year) Bleeding pattern before starting hormonal contraception, since starting and currently Other symptoms suggestive of an underlying cause