I</strong> – supplemental role.<br/><strong>-</strong> Intact junctional zone<br/><strong>B – HCG:</strong><br/>Help in management<br/>repeat after 48 hrs.</td><td>- treatment is as<strong> </strong>for tubal ectopic pregnancy<br/>- Hysteroscopy resection under USG or laparoscopic Guidance.</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Corn</strong><strong>u</strong><strong>al pregnancy</strong><br/><strong>1 /76,000</strong></td><td><strong>1. </strong>single interstitial portion of tube in the body of uterus.<br/>2. POC are mobile &amp; separate from uterus.<br/>3. Completely covered by myometrium.<br/>4. Vascular pedicle adjoining G.S to unicornate uterus.<br/>B – HCH help in management repeat after 48hours.</td><td><strong>- </strong>Excision of rudimentary horn.<strong> </strong><br/><strong>- </strong>Laparoscopy preferred.<strong> </strong></td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ovarian Ectopic:</strong><br/><strong>D/D</strong><br/>- Corpus Luteum<br/>- Tubal Ectopic<br/>- Ovarian Germ cell tumors.</td><td><strong>No specific criteria.</strong><br/>Negative sliding sign.<br/><em><strong>Diagnosis:</strong></em>  On histopathology<br/><em><strong>B – HCG:</strong></em> help in management repeat after 48hrs</td><td><strong>No selection Criteria</strong><br/><strong>Surgery: </strong>laparoscopically<br/>t/m of choice.<br/>- wedge resection – preferred.<br/>- Evacuation of POCS – to avoid ovarian damage.<br/>- Oophorectomy – in case of heavy bleeding or concurrent ovarian pathology.<br/><strong>Metho:</strong> 40% failure.</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Abdominal </strong><br/><strong>Ectopic:</strong><br/>1% of Ectopic pregnancies.<br/><strong>Types:</strong><br/><strong>1. </strong>Early before 20weeks.<br/><strong>2</strong>. Late after 20weeks.<br/><strong>Mortality:</strong><br/>- 7 Times of other ectopic.<br/>- 9 Times of I/U pregnancy.</td><td>1. Absence of Intra uterine G.S.<br/>2. Absence of adnexal mass.<br/>3. G.S surrounded by bowel loops.<br/>4. wide mobility to fluctuation of sac with TVS probe.<br/>5. No fistula<br/>6. No injury to tube/ovaries<br/><strong>MRI:</strong> Adjunctive role.<br/>- Help in management.<br/>- Locate placenta &amp; its relation with vital organs before t/m.<br/>Should be done in all cases.</td><td><strong>Surgical t/m:</strong><br/><strong>Laparoscopy: </strong>early ectopic except where implantation don’t involve vascular area.<br/><strong>Laparotomy: </strong>late ectopic<br/>placenta left in sites if attached e vital organs or vessels<br/>spontaneous resorption awaited.<br/><strong>Leaving placenta in</strong><strong> </strong><strong>sit</strong><strong>u</strong><br/><strong>But mortality is lower than Removing placentaBut mortality is lower than Removing placenta</strong>But mortality is lower than Removing placentaBut mortality is lower than Removing placenta<strong>- </strong>Risk of ileus<br/>But mortality is lower than Removing placenta<br/>But mortality is lower than Removing placenta<br/>- Intestinal<br/>obstruction<br/>- fistula formation<br/>- Peritonitis<br/>- Hemorrhage<br/><strong>Metho</strong>:<br/>- Adjuvant therapy with surgery.<br/>- Local ± systemic metho.<br/><strong>USG</strong> guided feticide.</td><td>angiographic embolization of vessels =&gt; can be offered as 1st line as it    es vacularity &amp; haemorrhage.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Heterotopic</strong><strong> pregnancy:</strong><br/><strong>Pt want pregnancy</strong><br/>- Surgery<br/><strong>Don’t want </strong><br/>- Medical<br/>- KCL<br/>- Glucose<br/>- Metho</td><td><strong>Suspected in:</strong><br/>- Assisted reproduction.<br/>- I/U pregnancy + persistent pelvic pain,<br/>- Persistent    ed B – HCG after miscarriage or TOP.<br/><strong>B – HCG:</strong><br/>Limited value.<br/>No role.</td><td><strong>Metho:</strong><br/><strong> </strong>If I/U pregnancy is non-viable.<br/>Women don’t wish to retain,<br/><strong>Local injections:</strong><br/>Of KCL or glucose with aspiration of G.S contents