<p>Women.Pregnancy is - who are tryingconfirmed.</p>
<p>to conceive. - No prophylaxis</p>
<p>required to prevent PPT.</p>', 3) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET content=EXCLUDED.content, type=EXCLUDED.type;
INSERT INTO topics (id, chapter_id, title, description, "order", is_published, author, source) VALUES ('t-mm-08-048', 'chap-mm-08-01', '39. VITAMIN D IN PREGNANCY', 'Detailed medical reference and study guide on 39. VITAMIN D IN PREGNANCY.', 48, true, 'Dr. Farzana Muneer', 'Maternal Mind FCPS/MRCOG Textbook') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET title=EXCLUDED.title, description=EXCLUDED.description, is_published=true;
INSERT INTO content_blocks (id, topic_id, type, content, "order") VALUES ('cb-5f09d403', 't-mm-08-048', 'html', '<ul><li>Daily requirement during pregnancy &amp; lactation 6000IU/day.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Maternal/Fetal Complications:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>PTE &amp; SGA.</li><li>LBW.</li><li>Impaired glucose tolerance – ethnic based association.</li><li>P0 C/section (vitamin D &lt; 37.5 =&gt;   risk of C section).</li><li>Bacterial vaginosis.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Neonatal complications:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Seizures: major cause is vitamin D deficiency.</li><li>Hypocalcemia seizures also due to vitamin D deficiency (25% mother has deficiency).</li></ul>
<h3><em><strong>Skeletal development &amp; Growth:</strong></em></h3>
<ul><li>25(OH) level in mother =&gt; reduced BMD in offspring.</li></ul>
<h3><em><strong>Lu</strong></em><em><strong>ng development &amp; immune disorders:</strong></em></h3>
<ul><li>25 (OH) levels in mother =&gt;</li><li>wheeze + asthma</li><li>bronchiolitis.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Screening for Vitamin D Deficiency in pregnancy:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>no role of universal or selective screening as treatment is safe.</li><li>Testing should be done in women with:</li><li>Low calcium.</li><li>Bone pain.</li><li>GI disease.</li><li>Alcohol abuse.</li><li>Previous child with Rickets.</li><li>Drug intake that    vitamin D levels.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Supplementation &amp; Treatment in Pregnancy:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>10ug/day</li><li>cholecalciferol or Ergocalciferol safe.</li><li><strong>Categories</strong><strong> of Vitamin D supplementation:</strong></li><li><em><strong>General:</strong></em></li><li>All pregnant population.</li><li>10ug, 400 units/ day.</li><li>All women (NICE).</li></ul>
<h3><strong>High Risk:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Obese</li><li>Socially excluded.</li><li>Dark skin</li><li>Reduced sun exposure.</li><li>1000IU/ day.</li><li>Risk of PTE =&gt; 800IU/ day + calcium.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Treatment of vitamin D deficiency:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>4 – 6 weeks’ treatment.</li><li>20,000IU Cholecalciferol/ week.</li><li>10,000 IU Ergo twice/ week.</li><li>4 – 6 weeks’ treatment followed by</li><li>1000IU/day maintenance dose.</li><li>Very high doses 300,000 – 500,000IU.</li><li>Risk of fracture so C/I during pregnancy.</li></ul>', 1) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET content=EXCLUDED.content, type=EXCLUDED.type;
