INSERT INTO content_blocks (id, topic_id, type, content, "order") VALUES ('cb-a0a4f4d5', 't-mm-10-028', 'html', '<h3><strong>1.2.3.4. 1.2.3.4. </strong><strong>1.</strong><strong>2.</strong><strong>3.</strong><strong>4. </strong><strong>1.</strong><strong>2.</strong><strong>3.</strong><strong>4. </strong></h3>
<h3><strong>WhiteAmberRedWhiteAmberRed</strong><strong>White</strong><strong>Amber</strong><strong>Red</strong><strong>White</strong><strong>Amber</strong><strong>Red</strong></h3>
<h3><strong>Important Consideration while Reviewing CTG:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Evaluate changes on trace over time</li><li>Document any change from previous review</li><li>Review changes alongside existing or new intrapartum risk factors</li><li>Think about possible reason for any change and take into account whole clinical picture in formulating ongoing care.</li><li>Normal CTG trace indicate that baby is coping well with labour</li><li>Changes to FHR during labour are common and don’t necessarily cause concern, however they may represent fetal compromise.</li><li>If CTG trace changes or is not normal there will be less certainly about the condition of the baby</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Accelerations:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Transient    in FHR of 15bpm or more lasting 15 sec or more</li><li>Presence of accelerations even with reduced variability is reassuring.</li><li>Absence of accelerations on an otherwise normal CTG trace does not indicate fetal acidosis.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Categorization of CTG traces:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Normal</li><li>No amber or no red feature</li><li>All 4 features are white.</li><li>Suspicious:</li><li>Any 1 feature amber</li><li>Pathological</li><li>Any feature red or</li><li>2 or more features are amber.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Special consideration for CTG traces in 2</strong><strong>nd</strong><strong> stage of labour:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Interpretation more challenging in 2nd stage</li><li>Keep low threshold for seeking 2nd opinion</li><li>Differentiate FHR from MHR every 5 min</li><li>Consider scalp electrode if there is concern</li><li>about confusing FHR and MHR.</li><li>Accelerations are most likely to be maternal pulse</li><li>If there are deceleration, look for other signs of hypoxia like   in baseline FHR or reduction in variability.</li><li>Hypoxia is more common and more rapid in active 2nd stage</li><li>FHR &gt; 20bPm from baseline or more from start of labour is labelled as red feature in active.</li><li>CTG concern in 2nd stage.</li><li>Obtain obstetric review</li><li>Discourage Pushing</li><li>Discontinue oxytocin infusion unless birth is imminent</li><li>Agree and document CTG trace.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Management of Abnormal CTG Trace:</strong></h3>
<h3><strong>Suspicious CTG:</strong></h3>
<h3><em><strong>1.</strong></em><em><strong>No other concerning risk Factor:</strong></em></h3>
<ul><li>Full assessment of clinical picture</li><li>Under take conservative measures.</li></ul>
<p><em><strong>2.</strong></em><em><strong>Other concerning risk factor like sepsis, meconium, slow progress.</strong></em></p>
<ul><li>Full assessment of clinical picture</li><li>Conservative measures</li><li>Urgent review by an obstetrician or a senior midwife</li><li>Consider</li><li>Fetal scalp stimulation</li><li>Expedite the birth.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Pathological CTG:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Urgent review by an obstetrician or senior midwife.</li><li>Exclude acute events</li><li>Cord prolapses</li><li>Abruption</li><li>Rupture</li><li>Full assessment</li><li>Undertake conservative measures</li><li>Still pathological after conservative measures</li><li>Obtain further urgent review</li><li>Evaluate whole clinical picture</li><li>Expedite birth</li><li>Document the timing of seeking review and making decision.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Acute Bradycardia or single Prolonged Deceleration for 3 min or more:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Urgent obstetric review</li><li>Acute event – Expedite birth</li><li>Underlying causes – undertake conservative measures</li><li>Make preparations for urgent birth including a request for pediatric or neonatal support</li></ul>
<h3><strong>[</strong><strong>Bradycardia</strong><strong>]</strong><strong> </strong></h3>
<ul><li>persists for 9 min or less in women with Risk Factor</li></ul>
<p>Expedite Birth</p>
<h3><strong>[</strong><strong>Bradycardia</strong><strong>]</strong></h3>
<ul><li>recovers up to 9 min</li></ul>
<p>Reassessment.</p>
<p>(P – T – O)</p>
<h3><strong>Underlying causes and conservative Measures</strong></h3>
<ul><li><strong>Stop oxytocinTocolytics terbutaline 0.25mg S/C.Stop oxytocinTocolytics terbutaline 0.25mg S/C.</strong>Stop oxytocinTocolytics terbutaline 0.25mg S/C.Stop oxytocinTocolytics terbutaline 0.25mg S/C.<strong>Left lateralI/V fluids if women are hypotensive secondary to epidural top – up.Call an anesthetistLeft lateralI/V fluids if women are hypotensive secondary to epidural top – up.Call an anesthetist</strong>Left lateralI/V fluids if women are hypotensive secondary to epidural top – up.Call an anesthetistLeft lateralI/V fluids if women are hypotensive secondary to epidural top – up.Call an anesthetist<strong>Maternal Position</strong><strong>hypotension</strong><strong>Excessive Contractions</strong></li></ul>
<p>Can cause</p>
<ul><li>Cord compression</li><li>Uterine blood flow</li></ul>
<p>Encourage to mobilize or</p>
<p>Adopt an alternate position</p>
<ul><li>No facial oxygen therapy</li><li>No amnioinfusion.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Fetal Scalp Stimulation</strong><strong>:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Suspicious CTG with antenatal or intrapartum risk factors for compromise</li><li>Digital scalp stimulation- No acceleration</li><li>Acceleration in FHR + improvement- Expedite birth</li><li>Continue FHR monitoring</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Record keeping for CTG:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Set date and time clock correctly on machine</li><li>Set paper speed at 1cm/ min</li><li>Correct labelling with women’s name, DOB, NHS Number, Pulse rate @ start of monitoring and date of CTG</li><li>Save record Electronically or photocopy of CTG trace</li><li>Keep record for 25 years</li><li>Sustained possible brain injury to baby =&gt; store CTG indefinitely.</li></ul>', 4) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET content=EXCLUDED.content, type=EXCLUDED.type;
INSERT INTO content_blocks (id, topic_id, type, content, "order") VALUES ('cb-ba34b0df', 't-mm-10-028', 'heading', '5. EXTERNAL CEPHALIC VERSION:ECV', 5) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET content=EXCLUDED.content, type=EXCLUDED.type;
INSERT INTO content_blocks (id, topic_id, type, content, "order") VALUES ('cb-8d3ce380', 't-mm-10-028', 'html', '<ul><li>in multigravida done @37 weeks</li><li>in Primigravida @ 36 weeks</li></ul>
<p>-increased Interval between ECV and birth –    chances of C/S</p>
<ul><li>-9% patient decline ECV</li><li>Successful – 50%:</li><li>Primi – 40%</li><li>Multi – 60%</li><li>Reversion to breach after ECV = 3%</li><li>ed risk of C/S due to obstructed labour and fetal distress in ECV pregnancies and    risk of instrumental birth.</li><li><strong>Tocolysis</strong> with Betamimetics (0.25mg Terbutaline) or 250ug salbutamol in 25ml saline I/V x slowly</li><li>Not effective in β blocker users.</li><li>(no role of nifedipine &amp; atosiban)</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Indication of tocolysis:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>1. Routinely</li><li>2. Tense abdomen</li><li>3. Unsuccessful ECV without tocolysis.</li><li>Prophylactic anti – D in D-ve women.</li><li>Regional/ Neuraxial anaesthesia not recommended routinely, used in repeat attempt or when women don’t tolerate</li></ul>
<p><strong>Unstable lie:</strong> ECV + stabilizing induction</p>
<h3><strong>Contraindication</strong><strong> of ECV</strong><strong>:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Reasons for C/S</li><li>Previa</li><li>Distress</li><li>Multiple pregnancy</li><li>Abruption</li><li>Severe PE</li><li>Abnormal Doppler</li><li>Bleeding &lt; 1 week</li><li>PROM</li><li>Mother declines</li><li>Myomectomy</li><li>Electronic fetal monitoring is recommended after ECV</li><li>Testing for Fetomaternal haemorrhage is recommended</li><li>Up to 4 attempts in 10 min</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Caution:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Previous C/S</li><li>Polyhydramnios</li><li>Oligohydramnios</li><li>HTN</li></ul>
<h3><strong>No ECV once Contraction Started:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Except twins</li><li>ECV=2nd twin + intact membrane</li><li>IPV = twin + Ruptured membrane</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Pre requisites</strong><strong> of ECV</strong><strong>:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Keep in hand USG, CEFM, OT, Anaesthesia</li><li>Transient Bradycardia up to 3 min is normal</li><li>6 min – cat1 C/S</li><li>Abnormal persistent CTG</li><li>Abdominal painurgent delivery</li><li>Unexplained Bleeding</li><li>Risk of emergency C/S – 0.5%, 1/200</li><li>90% due to vaginal bleeding and fetal distress.</li><li>Moxibustions – Chinese Medicine     fetal activity</li></ul>
<h3><em><strong>Consider:</strong></em></h3>
<ul><li>@ 33 – 35 weeks for 2 weeks</li><li>43 % success @ 32 weeks.</li><li>Postural management – no evidence.</li></ul>', 6) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET content=EXCLUDED.content, type=EXCLUDED.type;
INSERT INTO topics (id, chapter_id, title, description, "order", is_published, author, source) VALUES ('t-mm-10-029', 'chap-mm-10-01', '5. ECV', 'Detailed medical reference and study guide on 5. ECV.', 29, true, 'Dr. Farzana Muneer', 'Maternal Mind FCPS/MRCOG Textbook') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET title=EXCLUDED.title, description=EXCLUDED.description, is_published=true;
INSERT INTO topics (id, chapter_id, title, description, "order", is_published, author, source) VALUES ('t-mm-10-030', 'chap-mm-10-01', '6.BREECH PRESENTATION', 'Detailed medical reference and study guide on 6.BREECH PRESENTATION.', 30, true, 'Dr. Farzana Muneer', 'Maternal Mind FCPS/MRCOG Textbook') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET title=EXCLUDED.title, description=EXCLUDED.description, is_published=true;
INSERT INTO content_blocks (id, topic_id, type, content, "order") VALUES ('cb-df583fa2', 't-mm-10-030', 'html', '<ul><li>3 – 4% @ Term</li><li>20 – 32% undetected</li><li>Detection by abdominal examination = 70%</li><li>Recurrence of Breech – 9.9%</li><li>offer ECV to all women (except with C/I)</li><li>Explain Risk/ benefits of ECV</li><li>Women declining ECV =&gt; Offer vaginal Breech delivery Risk, benefits Vs elective C/S risk, benefits</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Perinatal Mortality:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>SVD = 1/1000</li><li>Vaginal Breech = 2/1000</li><li>C/S = 0.5/1000</li></ul>
<h3><strong>C/S is Recommended in:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Hyper extended Neck</li><li>EFW &gt; 3.8</li><li>EFW &lt; 10th centile</li><li>Footling Breech</li><li>Antenatal fetal compromise</li><li>Unavailability of experienced breech Birth attendant.</li><li>Expert/ Skilled birth attendant</li><li>Not available – refer to unit where TBA is available</li><li>No unit available</li><li>C – Section.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Planned Vaginal Birth:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Risk 0f</li><li>Short term complications</li><li>Low APGAR</li><li>No    risk of long-term complications.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Breech in labour:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Need for emergency C/S = 45%</li></ul>
<h3><em><strong>1</strong></em><em><strong>st</strong></em><em><strong> stage: </strong></em></h3>
<ul><li>check for risk factor, expertise, available USG for EFW, leg and neck position – women preferences.</li></ul>
<h3><em><strong>II</strong></em><em><strong>0</strong></em><em><strong> Stage:</strong></em></h3>
<ul><li>Vaginal birth</li><li>C/S =&gt; 2 hours after passive II0 stage.</li><li>IOL avoided</li><li>Augmentation for slow progress with epidural &lt; 4 contraction/10min is considered</li><li>Continuous Fetal monitoring improve outcome.</li><li>FBS avoided</li><li>Positions = according to women’s preferences</li><li>Semi recurrent</li><li>All four</li><li>Dorsal lithotomy</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Delivery:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Without traction hands off</li><li>Avoid or minimize tactile stimulation because it results in extension of arm and neck making delivery more difficult.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Assisted Delivery if:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>5min from delivery of breech to head OR &gt; 3min umbilicus to Head.</li><li>Poor tone</li><li>Color change</li><li>Poor fetal condition</li><li>Extended arms/ neck</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Delivery of head:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Forceps</li><li>M – S Veit Maneuver</li></ul>
<p>Avoid burns Marchel’s Maneuver.</p>
<h3><strong>Pre Term</strong><strong> Breech:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Elective CS if birth is planned for maternal/ fetal compromise.</li><li>Pre – term Breech in labor =&gt; vaginal birth</li><li><strong>Head entrapm</strong><strong>ent</strong></li><li><em><strong>C/section</strong></em> – J shape or invented T</li><li><em><strong>Vaginal Birth</strong></em> – cervical incisions 2,6,10 ‘O clock with or without tocolysis</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Twins’</strong><strong> 1</strong><strong>st</strong><strong> Breech:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Locked Twin 1/817</li><li>Elective CS &gt; 37weeks.</li><li>In labour – emergency CS – Not recommended</li><li>Assess clinically</li><li><em><strong>2</strong></em><em><strong>nd</strong></em><em><strong> Breech</strong></em> 40% incidence</li></ul>', 1) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET content=EXCLUDED.content, type=EXCLUDED.type;
