<ul><li>Hypoxia</li><li>Hypovolemia</li><li>Hypo/hyperkalemia – hyponatremia – oxytocin use</li><li>Hypothermia.</li><li>Hydrogen ions (acidosis)</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Other Considerations</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Manual uterine displacement or left lateral tilt</li><li>Empty uterus if &gt; 20weeks</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Important points regarding CPR;</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Reversible cause – survival is more</li><li>Non pregnant – laryngeal airway or supraglottic airway are acceptable but not in pregnant it is not suitable due to risk of regurgitation, mucosal bleeding and further intubation is difficulty due to blood and obscured view.</li><li>Abdominal USG by expert to look for concealed hemorrhage.</li><li>Vasovagal &amp; epilepsy =&gt; commonest cause of collapse.</li><li>Manual uterine displacement or left lateral tilt</li><li>Perimortem C/S</li><li>If &gt;20 weeks</li><li>Aim for uterine evacuation within 5 min</li><li>No response despite correct performance of CPR within 4 min</li><li>Achieved within 5 min</li><li>69% infant survival</li><li>Hypoxic brain damage of neonate if perimortem C/S performed after 6min.</li><li>Cardiac arrest 50% survival of mother.</li><li>54% survived until discharge</li><li>42% with mod – good Neurological outcomes.</li><li>More favorable outcome when CPR performed within 10min.</li></ul>
